@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: which bytes arriving on stdin are a terminal ANSWERING, and which of those an agent may see
' covers: internal/ptyproxy/inputfilter.go, internal/ptyproxy/inputfilter_test.go
' covers: internal/ptyproxy/ptyproxy.go, internal/ptyproxy/ptyproxy_test.go
' covers: cmd/proveo/main.go
'   the pty the filter sits on ..... _spec/internal/reviewgate/pty-review-proxy.puml
'   the tap that recorded all this . _spec/internal/agentio/agent-terminal-io.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── THE RECOGNISED SET IS CLOSED, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IS A KEYSTROKE ───────
' `classifyTerminalReport` names the sequences it knows and calls everything
' else `reportNone`, which is forwarded. The asymmetry is deliberate and runs
' the safe way: an unrecognised REPORT reaching the agent is the bug this file
' documents, and an unrecognised KEYSTROKE eaten is a worse one. Every gap found
' so far — the kitty pair, the mouse encodings — was closed by ADDING a case,
' never by widening a default.
'
' The whole set, so a future gap is visible as an absence rather than guessed at:
'   reportFocus ... `CSI I` / `CSI O`, a body of exactly one byte (DEC 1004).
'   reportReply ... `CSI ? … c` and `CSI > … c` — Primary/Secondary Device
'                   Attributes; `CSI ? … $ y` — DECRPM, the answer to a mode
'                   query; `CSI <digits;> R` — the cursor position report;
'                   `CSI ? <flags> u` — the kitty keyboard flag report;
'                   `DCS … ST` — XTVERSION and friends; `APC … ST` — the kitty
'                   GRAPHICS answer; `OSC … ST`-or-BEL — colour and palette.
'   reportMouse ... `CSI M` plus exactly three more bytes (X10), `CSI < … M|m`
'                   (SGR), and `CSI <digits;> M` (urxvt).
' The parameter guards are load-bearing in one direction only: they exist so a
' real key is never classified as an answer, never the reverse.
'
' ── A SEQUENCE IS FOUND BY ITS TERMINATOR, NEVER BY A LENGTH ───────────────
' `escEnd` recognises the terminator each introducer actually uses: CSI runs its
' parameters up to a final byte in 0x40..0x7e; DCS, OSC, APC and PM (`ESC P`,
' `ESC ]`, `ESC _`, `ESC ^`) run to ST (`ESC \`) or BEL (0x07). ANYTHING ELSE IS
' TWO BYTES — `ESC` plus one — an Alt-keypress or an introducer proveo does not
' parse. Scanning on past an unknown introducer instead is how one stray ESC
' would swallow the rest of the buffer, keystrokes included.
'
' Incomplete is a third answer, not a rounding of the other two: the fragment is
' neither forwarded nor dropped, it is HELD for the next read.
'
' ── THE WINDOW IS A STATEMENT ABOUT WHO ASKED, NOT ABOUT WHEN ──────────────
' `DefaultReplyWindow` (2s) is how close together two IDENTICAL reports must
' arrive to read as one terminal answering twice rather than as the application
' asking twice. Further apart is a genuine second query, and a genuine second
' query is owed a genuine second answer — so the window is a claim about
' authorship, and the 2s is only how long that claim stays plausible.
'
' ── THE VERBATIM EVIDENCE THE NOTES BELOW COMPRESS ─────────────────────────
' opencode 1.18.29 opens with BOTH kitty probes. The graphics one goes out as
'   ESC _ Gi=31337,s=1,v=1,a=q,t=d,f=24;AAAA ESC \
' and the keyboard one as `CSI > 5 u`; the terminal's answers to each were
' `reportNone` and arrived as typing.
'
' The read that ended a real crashed session, byte for byte:
'   ^[[200~sk-…^[[201~cERROR: sandbox "…" was stopped
' A bracketed paste, then a lone `c` — the tail of a DA1 reply whose head came
' in the previous read.
'
title Terminal reports — filtered before they become prompts

node "Operator's terminal stack" as TERM <<host>> {
  component "terminal emulator" as EMU <<host>>
  component "multiplexer\nZellij · tmux · screen" as MUX <<error>>
}

node "proveo — ptyproxy" as PX <<host>> {
  component "**classify** — ""classifyTerminalReport""\nlone, self-delimiting reports only" as CLASS <<app>>
  component "**filter** — ""inputFilter.keep""\nwindowed de-duplication" as FILT <<app>>
  component "**tap** — ""PROVEO_TRACE_STDIN""\nrecords arrived + verdict" as TAP <<async>>
}

node "sbx backend — ""DropReports = true""" as SBX <<cloud>> {
  component "agent-session API\ninput read as a PROMPT STREAM" as SESS <<cloud>>
}
node "docker backend · review gate" as DOCK <<app>> {
  component "agent on a REAL tty\nreads reports, discards what it did not ask for" as TTY <<app>>
}
component "Claude Code" as AGENT <<app>>

EMU ARROW_MAIN MUX : keystrokes · query replies
MUX ARROW_ERROR CLASS : one query, TWO answers — or one nobody asked for
CLASS ARROW_MAIN FILT
FILT ARROW_MAIN SBX : keystrokes ONLY — every report dropped
FILT ARROW_MAIN DOCK : keystrokes + the first of each reply
FILT ARROW_QUEUE TAP : every read, kept or dropped
SBX ARROW_MAIN SESS
SESS ARROW_MAIN AGENT
TTY ARROW_MAIN AGENT
SESS ARROW_ERROR AGENT : an unfiltered report arrives as ""promptSource: sdk""

note bottom of MUX
  **Measured, not assumed.** In run ""proveo-1787703005-59852"" the tap recorded
  Zellij answering ONE Primary Device Attributes query twice — two identical
  ""\\x1b[?62;4c"" reads 42ms apart — plus focus reports nothing downstream consumes.

  The surplus has no query to belong to. Whatever reads the stream must treat it
  as input, because input is the only thing it can be.

  **A duplicate was never the requirement.** Run ""proveo-1787852436-14907"" died
  the same way with no duplicate anywhere: the tap recorded ONE ""\\x1b[?6c"" —
  a VT102 Device Attributes reply — five seconds into a run, ""sent"", while the
  focus reports 3s and 8s later were both ""DROPPED"". A lone report is
  indistinguishable from a legitimate answer under de-duplication alone, so it
  was forwarded as "the first copy" and enqueued. The agent answered a ""/color""
  nobody typed and exited; the tail's last bytes were the report's trailing ""c"".

  This needs no multiplexer and no second instance. One unsolicited report is
  the whole mechanism.

  **And reports are not the only shape of it.** The same tap on run
  ""proveo-1787868039-64880"" recorded 65 SGR mouse-motion reads —
  ""\x1b[<35;1;46M"" through ""\x1b[<35;50;39M"", coordinates drifting as a hand
  moved across the window — every one ""sent"", because ""classifyTerminalReport""
  knew nothing ending in ""M"". On a prompt stream they land in the input line as
  noise. Mouse tracking was enabled BY the agent, so this is a report the far end
  asked for and cannot receive: solicited at one end of the hop and meaningless
  at the other.
end note

note right of SESS
  **Why an ignorable stray becomes a user message.** A terminal application
  discards a report it did not ask for. sbx does not run the agent as a terminal
  application: it drives it over the agent-session API, where input is a PROMPT
  STREAM. The transcript records the result as
  ""promptSource: sdk · entrypoint: sdk-cli"" — enqueued, dequeued, answered.

  It never reaches ""history\.jsonl"", because that records what the TUI submitted
  and the TUI submitted nothing. That absence is how the injection was found.
end note

note left of FILT
  **Three rules, and the first one belongs to the BACKEND.**

  ""dropReplies"" (""Proxy.DropReports"") — drop EVERY report. Set on the sbx
  path and nowhere else. De-duplication cannot cover that backend, and the
  reason is that it answers a different question: it decides which COPY is
  surplus, having already granted that the first one is owed. On a prompt
  stream nothing was ever asked, so the first copy is already one too many.

  ""reportReply"" (default) — forward the FIRST, drop copies inside
  ""DefaultReplyWindow"" (2s). The application asked and is owed one answer;
  only the surplus goes. De-duplication is windowed rather than
  adjacent-only, because the captured duplicate had a mode report between
  it and its twin. This is the DOCKER contract, where the agent holds a real
  tty and a withheld answer leaves it waiting.

  ""reportFocus"" — dropped outright. A focus notification is neither a
  keystroke nor an answer, and nothing past this point consumes one. That it
  was unconditional is why focus never caused this and replies did.

  ""reportMouse"" — DEC 1000/1006/1015/1016, in all four encodings. SOLICITED,
  unlike the other two: the application turned tracking on, and a TUI on a real
  tty consumes clicks and scroll. So it follows ""dropReplies"" and is never
  de-duplicated — every motion is a distinct event, not a surplus copy of one.
  ""CSI M Cb Cx Cy"" is matched on its LENGTH because its coordinates are raw
  bytes; the other three end in ""M"" or ""m"" and match like the rest.

  A read carrying a report ALONGSIDE anything else is forwarded untouched.
  Eating a keystroke to win a filtering argument is far worse than passing a
  stray report through — and terminals emit reports as one atomic write, which
  is what makes the whole-read rule safe.

  ""PROVEO_STDIN_FILTER=off"" restores byte-for-byte passthrough.
end note

note bottom of TAP
  The tap exists because a child-side log cannot show what was DROPPED, and a
  transcript cannot show what ARRIVED. Wrapping ""os.Stdin"" to find out would
  make ""os/exec"" substitute a pipe and take the agent's tty away — changing the
  very behaviour under investigation. Through the pty the child keeps its
  terminal either way.
end note
note as N_KITTY_GAP
  **Two replies it did not know, and opencode
  provokes BOTH on startup.** A kitty GRAPHICS
  probe (APC) and a kitty KEYBOARD push
  (""CSI > 5 u""), each answered by the terminal.
end note

note as N_KITTY_APC
  ""ESC _ Gi=31337;OK ESC\\"" is APC — ""b[1] == '_'""
  was simply absent from the switch, so the
  answer classified as ""reportNone"" and reached
  the agent as keystrokes.
end note

note as N_KITTY_U
  ""ESC [ ? <flags> u"" had no ""'u'"" case either.
  The guard is ""body[0] == '?'"": a kitty-encoded
  KEYPRESS is ""CSI 1;5 u"" with no '?', and
  swallowing real keys would be the worse bug.
end note

note as N_KITTY_SEEN
  Observed in a live session: the pane filling
  with ""Gi=31337,…"", ""^[[18~"" (F7), ""^[[19~"" (F8)
  and bare digits — terminal answers arriving as
  input.
end note

note as N_KITTY_TAIL
  And every crash ended ""cERROR: sandbox … was
  stopped"" — that stray ""c"" is the tail of a DA1
  reply whose prefix went elsewhere. Three
  sightings before it was read as a clue.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_UNIT
  **The filter judges a SEQUENCE, not a read.**
  ""keep()"" took one whole ""Read()"" and answered
  yes or no for all of it, which is wrong in two
  directions at once.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_TAIL
  A reply split across reads left a fragment:
  ""ESC[?62;"" then ""c"". The second piece failed
  the ""len < 3"" guard and reached the agent as a
  keystroke.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_SEEN
  That ""c"" sat in front of every ""sandbox was
  stopped"" in this investigation — seen three
  times and dismissed three times before it was
  read as evidence.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_BOTH
  And a read carrying a report BESIDE real typing
  was all-or-nothing, so filtering it ate the
  keystrokes too.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_PASTE
  Bracketed paste makes both routine: the text,
  its ""ESC[200~""/""ESC[201~"" markers and whatever
  the terminal was still answering all land in
  one read.
end note

note as N_SPLIT_HOLD
  An unfinished sequence is HELD for the next
  read, bounded by ""maxHeld"" (128B) so a lone ESC
  keypress is never swallowed and no run can
  hoard the buffer.
end note

@enduml
